"""Technical Q&A shared with the group rehearsal document."""
QUESTIONS=[('Why do five pipeline stages not mean five global execution states?', 'RUN can persist while different instructions occupy all five stages. The global modes describe running, draining and stopping. Local actions depend on each slot and cross-stage decisions.'), ('What is the difference between a control intention and an enable?', 'Decode creates an intention such as reg_write. Validity, fault status, reset and mode qualify it at the action stage. rf_we is the resulting writeback enable.'), ('Why does load-to-store need a stall in default AC1?', 'Both store operands are prepared in EX. An adjacent load returns data only at the end of MEM. The store waits one cycle, then forwards ready W data into EX. The WB-to-MEM candidate uses a different contract.'), ('Why does output reg not necessarily imply flip-flops?', 'A fully assigned combinational always block describes combinational logic. The Verilog variable type permits procedural assignment. State requires clocked storage or incomplete combinational assignment.'), ('What do carry and overflow mean?', 'For ADD, C is the unsigned carry out. For SUB, C means no borrow. V detects signed overflow. ADD and SUB update C/V, CMP updates Z, and memory-address arithmetic preserves architectural flags.'), ('Where do LDR, STR and CMP calculate their results?', 'LDR and STR use the ALU for base plus signed displacement, preserving flags. CMP uses the independent equality path on forwarded operands and writes Z in WB.'), ('Why does CMP-to-JZ need no register data stall?', 'JZ selects the newest eligible CMP Z candidate from M, then W, then saved Z. CMP still updates architectural Z at WB. The branch uses the candidate as a condition.'), ('How does synchronous memory avoid adding a sixth stage?', 'The registered IM output is the ID instruction data boundary. The registered DM output supplies the WB load value. The existing tag registers align with those outputs. No extra IR or MDR is cascaded there.'), ('How independent is the reference model?', 'It implements ISA effects separately from pipeline control and is cross-checked against explicit boundary vectors. Timing assertions check implementation-specific events separately. Shared specification errors remain possible, so finite passing tests are not a full proof.'), ('What exactly did the board verify?', 'Five normal-HALT programs, each with two stable VIO snapshots, plus physical S1 reset/restart after stopping. RF observations are writeback shadows. Fault paths and internal cycle traces retain their simulation evidence.'), ('Why can a longer program have a lower CPI?', 'Fixed filling overhead is amortized across more completed instructions, and instruction mixes incur different stalls and redirects. Compare the work and overhead, not total cycles alone.'), ('Is the CPU execution time directly measured?', 'Cycle counters are physically observed. Converting them to nanoseconds assumes the designed 100 MHz clock. Independent frequency, wall-time and power measurements were not performed.')]
